Heres a pattern, we have this new technology X that is really cool. Its new therefore untested/unverified but we tell you its
the Golden Boy for XYZ problem. The old MNO solution is hard / not easy to maintain and blablabla.

The pattern is that of vilifying an existing solution, creating FUD about it and proposing a highly complicated solution that proposes
to fix the old problem.

Ideally you would like the logic to be:
<a> We are in the business of solving problem XYZ

<b> Solution ABC in use tries to solve the same problem.

    -> Now either it doesnt solve it satisfactorily... provide proof 
       -> Proof that the unsatisfactoriness is contingent upon the solution ABC not one of its implementations
     OR
    -> It solves the problem (its hard to prove otherwise but thers some amt of generally accepted uneasiness) and
       above all it brings way tooooo much complexity.

<c> Our spanking new solution 123 solves the same problem.
    -> Prove it solves *NO LESS* 
     AND
    -> Prove it fixes the remnant complexities of the previous solution

Heres Brian Cantrill going on about STM banging against shared memoryh locking and I think to a large extent he has a point:
"Again, no data, no qualifiers, no study, no rationale, no evidence of experience trying to develop such systems -- just a naked assertion used as a prop for a complicated and dubious solution"